<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    Notification small icon.

    NOT ic_launcher_foreground. An adaptive-icon foreground carries a large
    built-in safe-zone margin — ours draws its artwork at 0.7 scale inside a
    108dp canvas, so the glyph covers only ~54% of the box. Android renders a
    small icon by taking the ALPHA channel and drawing it at 24dp, margin and
    all, which is why the launcher foreground shows up visibly shrunken in the
    status bar next to apps that ship a real notification asset.

    This is the same artwork re-fitted: the bounding box (x 9.8..92.9,
    y 3.4..79.1) is scaled 1.107x and re-centred to fill 92% of the viewport,
    leaving only a small optical margin.

    Every path is #FFFFFF because colour is discarded — the system keeps the
    alpha and tints the result (white in the status bar, the channel/accent
    colour in the shade). The eye cut-outs come from the clip-path, so they stay
    transparent rather than becoming white blobs, which is what keeps the mark
    readable at 24dp.
-->
